From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niklas Sheth <hi@niklassheth.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 16:15:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: shrink the render FList ring and make the
 pool size a knob

Each VM that renders gets a render support object whose FList (free
list) holds 24 operand buffers of 2 MiB plus a ring directory of 512Ki
entries (4 MiB), all allocated at its first render: 52 of the 54 MiB of
private render storage per client. The accelerator takes 4 KiB pages
from this pool for shader private memory (scratch frames: spills and
dynamically indexed arrays) and returns them to the ring tail when the
work retires (support +0x20 counts returns, +0x24 is the tail).

The ring only ever holds the pool's own pages. Size it to twice the
registered pages (24576 entries, 192 KiB), as the compute support
object keeps its ring above its pages, with the tail at the page count.
The firmware wraps the tail at that capacity; 32 KiB scratch frames
across full-screen draws, which cycle the ring many times, render
correctly at #302's speed. The unused directory span is an unbacked
hole the support object never advertises. This saves 3.8 MiB per
client: render private storage is 50.0 MiB instead of 53.8 MiB.

The 24 buffers stay. The pool size bounds how many scratch-using
threads the GPU can keep resident, and smaller pools starve large
frames (full-screen 1920x1080 draws, 32 KiB per invocation, mini):

  buffers  ms/draw  kernel log
  24        48.5    clean
  20        50.8    clean
  16        56.6    notification wait timed out (1 s flight)
   8       180.4    notification timeout, A4 wedge detection (2 s)
   4         -      render never completed; G16 failed until reboot

4 KiB frames got faster with smaller pools (24: 16.0 ms, 8: 8.0 ms), so
the size is a real trade-off, but a pool that can hang the GPU is not a
default. Keep 24 and expose the count as the research parameter
render_operand_buffers (1..=24; runtime: debugfs
asahi_m4/render_operand_buffers, read at each VM's first render).
Native mesh VMs always use 24. Unregistered buffers are unbacked holes;
the VA layout is unchanged.

fw_mem now includes the render support pages and the opening FList
support object, so their ring counters can be read.

No UAPI change.
